{"name":"New Harvest Moon","hex":"#eddfc7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"new","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Newar."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Recently made, or created.","Of recent origin; having taken place recently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/new"},{"term":"harvest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process of gathering the ripened crop; harvesting.","The yield of harvesting, i.e., the gathered crops or fruits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To bring in a harvest; reap; glean.","To take a living organism as part of a managed process to gather food or resources, often with the intention of maintaining a healthy population."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/harvest"},{"term":"moon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Moon (“the Earth's only permanent natural satellite”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any natural satellite of a planet.","The light received from the Moon; moonlight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Full moon","blocks":[{"text":"The full moon is the lunar phase when the Moon appears fully illuminated from Earth's perspective. This occurs when Earth is located between the Sun and the Moon. This means that the lunar hemisphere facing Earth—the near side—is completely sunlit and appears as an approximately circular disk. The full moon occurs roughly once a month."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Pournami.jpg/330px-Pournami.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_moon","wikidata":"Q104641"},{"title":"New Harvest","blocks":[{"text":"New Harvest is a donor-funded research institute dedicated to the field of cellular agriculture, focusing on advances in scientific research efforts surrounding cultured animal products. Its research aims to resolve growing environmental and ethical concerns associated with industrial livestock production."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/New-harvest-logo-update.svg/330px-New-harvest-logo-update.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Harvest","wikidata":"Q7008464"},{"title":"Harvest","blocks":[{"text":"Harvesting is the process of collecting plants, animals, or fish as food, especially the process of gathering mature crops, and \"the harvest\" also refers to the collected crops. Reaping is the cutting of grain or pulses for harvest, typically using a scythe, sickle, or reaper. On smaller farms with minimal mechanization, harvesting is the most labor-intensive activity of the growing season. On large mechanized farms, harvesting uses farm machinery, such as the combine harvester. Automation has increased the efficiency of both the seeding and harvesting processes. Specialized harvesting equipment, using conveyor belts for gentle gripping and mass transport, replaces the manual task of removing each seedling by hand. The term \"harvesting\" in general usage may include immediate postharvest handling, including cleaning, sorting, packing, and cooling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Agriculture_in_Volgograd_Oblast_002.JPG/330px-Agriculture_in_Volgograd_Oblast_002.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvest","wikidata":"Q213753"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon","wikidata":"Q405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/FullMoon2020.jpg/500px-FullMoon2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/FullMoon2020.jpg/1280px-FullMoon2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/FullMoon2020.jpg/500px-FullMoon2020.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A full moon was taken on August 3, 2020","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFullMoon2020.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg/500px-Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg/500px-Full_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Full moon, 13 March 2025. Taken with a Nikon Z6II camera and Sigma 150-600mm f 5-6.3 DG OS HSM lens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFull_moon%2C_March_13%2C_2025._Taken_with_a_Nikon_Z6II_camera_and_Sigma_150-600mm_f_5-6.3_DG_OS_HSM_lens.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg/500px-Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg/1280px-Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg/500px-Moon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A full moon rising, seen through the Belt of Venus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoon_and_red_blue_haze.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Harvest_moon.jpg/500px-Harvest_moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Harvest_moon.jpg/500px-Harvest_moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A harvest moon. Its orange color is due to greater Rayleigh scattering as the Moon appears close above the horizon, rather than being unique to harvest moons.[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHarvest_moon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif/330px-Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif/330px-Christmas_full_moons_1977-2091.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The December 2015 full moon coincided with Christmas.[44] This last occurred in 1977 (for the American time zones). A s